// Code-managed blog content. Entries with a slug that exists in the database override its fields;
// entries that do not exist in the database are published as new posts.
const i = (text) => ({ type: 'intro', text });
const h = (text) => ({ type: 'h2', text });
const h3 = (text) => ({ type: 'h3', text });
const p = (text) => ({ type: 'p', text });
const ul = (...items) => ({ type: 'ul', items });
const cta = (text, href) => ({ type: 'cta', text, href });

const author = { author_name: 'Csharptek Engineering', author_role: 'AI and Azure engineering team' };

export const blogPosts = {
  'vibe-coding-mvp-development': {
    title: 'Vibe Coding to Production: What Breaks and How to Fix It',
    meta_title: 'Vibe Coding to Production: What Breaks and How to Fix It',
    meta_description: 'Built an MVP with Lovable, Bolt or Cursor? Here is what breaks between a vibe-coded prototype and a production app, and the order to fix it in.',
    excerpt: 'AI coding tools get you to a working demo fast. These are the gaps that appear between the demo and a product real customers can rely on.',
    category: 'Product engineering', read_time: '8 min read', tags: ['vibe coding', 'MVP', 'prototype to production', 'AI coding tools'],
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('Tools like Lovable, Bolt, Cursor and Replit let a founder or product team build a working app in days. That speed is real, and so is the gap that follows: a vibe-coded prototype proves an idea, but it is rarely a product you can put customers and their data on.'),
      p('This guide covers what typically breaks between a vibe-coded MVP and a production system, how to tell how far yours is from ready, and the order we fix things in.'),
      h('Why vibe-coded apps work in the demo and fail in production'),
      p('AI coding tools optimise for a visible result. They generate screens, wire up a database and make the happy path work. They do not, by default, design for concurrent users, hostile input, partial failures, data growth or a team that must maintain the code in six months. The prototype is built for one user doing the expected thing. Production is many users doing unexpected things.'),
      h('The seven things that usually break'),
      h3('1. Authentication and authorisation'),
      p('Prototypes often have sign-in but weak access control. A common pattern is that the interface hides data a user should not see, while the API still returns it. Production needs access rules enforced on the server and in the database, role-based permissions, safe session handling and password reset flows that cannot be abused.'),
      h3('2. Data model and tenancy'),
      p('A schema generated to make a screen work tends to be hard to extend. If the product will serve multiple customers, every table needs a clear tenant boundary, and every query must respect it. Retrofitting tenancy after launch is expensive and risky, so check it early.'),
      h3('3. Secrets and configuration'),
      p('API keys committed to a repository, keys exposed in browser code, and one shared environment for testing and real data are all common. Production needs secrets in a managed store, separate environments, and keys with the narrowest permissions that work.'),
      h3('4. Error handling and resilience'),
      p('Calls to payment providers, email services and language models fail, time out and rate-limit. Prototype code usually assumes success. Production code retries safely, fails visibly, avoids duplicate charges or messages, and leaves the system in a consistent state.'),
      h3('5. Testing and deployment'),
      p('Without automated tests, every change risks breaking something that worked. Without a repeatable deployment, releases depend on one person and one laptop. A minimal pipeline with tests on the critical paths, staging, and one-step rollback removes most of that risk.'),
      h3('6. Observability'),
      p('When something goes wrong in production you need to know what happened. That means structured logs, error tracking, uptime checks and, for AI features, a trace of prompts, tool calls and outputs. Without these, support becomes guesswork.'),
      h3('7. Cost and performance of AI features'),
      p('Prototypes call the biggest model for everything and send far more context than needed. At real usage this is slow and expensive. Measuring tokens and latency per task, choosing smaller models where quality allows, and caching repeated work keeps unit economics workable.'),
      h('How to assess how far your prototype is from production'),
      p('You can run a quick review yourself. Answer these questions about your app:'),
      ul('Can one customer ever see another customer’s data through the API?', 'Are all secrets outside the codebase and the browser?', 'Do you have automated tests for sign-in, payments and your core workflow?', 'Can you deploy and roll back without manual steps?', 'Would you know within minutes if the app went down or an error spiked?', 'Do you know what each AI call costs and how long it takes?', 'Could another engineer understand and change the code?'),
      p('Each “no” is a piece of hardening work. Most vibe-coded apps have several.'),
      h('Fix, rework or rebuild?'),
      p('The honest answer depends on the code. Many prototypes can be hardened in place: add access control, tests, a pipeline and monitoring, then refactor the weakest parts. Some have a data model or structure that fights every change, and rebuilding the core while keeping the interface and learnings is cheaper than patching. A short code and architecture review decides which, and avoids paying for a rewrite you did not need.'),
      h('The order we fix things in'),
      ul('Security first: access control, secrets, and exposed data.', 'Then data: schema, tenancy and backups.', 'Then reliability: error handling, retries and idempotency.', 'Then delivery: tests, CI/CD and environments.', 'Then visibility: logging, tracing and alerts.', 'Then cost and performance tuning for AI calls.'),
      p('This order reduces the chance of a data leak or outage before it improves anything else. It also gives you something to show investors or customers quickly: a short list of what is now safe.'),
      h('Where AI coding tools still help'),
      p('Vibe coding is not the problem. It is a fast way to explore and to build the parts that are routine. We often keep using AI coding tools during hardening, with an engineer reviewing every change, and with tests in place so mistakes are caught.'),
      h('Red flags to look for in generated code'),
      ul('The same logic copied into many files, so one fix must be made in ten places', 'Database queries built by joining strings with user input', 'Access checks only in the front end', 'Environment values hard-coded in components', 'Dependencies that are unmaintained or unused', 'No separation between data access, business rules and screens', 'Long files that mix everything and have no tests'),
      p('None of these makes a prototype useless. Together they tell you how much of the code can be kept.'),
      h('A typical hardening plan, week by week'),
      h3('Week 1: review and secure'),
      p('Review the repository, data model and deployment. Rotate any exposed secrets, close access-control gaps and take a backup. Produce a written list of findings ranked by risk.'),
      h3('Week 2: stabilise the foundation'),
      p('Fix the schema and tenancy issues that block safe growth, add environments for testing and production, and set up error tracking and logging.'),
      h3('Weeks 3 and 4: tests and delivery'),
      p('Add automated tests around sign-in, payments and the core workflow, then a pipeline that runs them and deploys with rollback. Refactor the worst files while tests protect behaviour.'),
      h3('After that: scale and extend'),
      p('Tune AI cost and latency, add the features customers are waiting for, and keep a short backlog of technical debt that you retire steadily. The timeline varies with the size of the app and the number of gaps, so treat these weeks as a shape, not a quote.'),
      h('A common example: the permission bug'),
      p('A founder builds a customer portal where each client sees their own invoices. The page filters correctly, so every test looks right. But the API behind the page accepts an invoice ID and returns it without checking the owner. Anyone who changes the number in the URL sees another client’s invoice. This is one of the most frequent problems in generated apps, and it is invisible in a demo. Fixing it means checking ownership on the server for every request and adding tests that try to break the rule.'),
      h('What to keep using AI tools for'),
      ul('Boilerplate: forms, tables, simple endpoints', 'Test generation, reviewed by an engineer', 'Documentation and code explanation', 'Refactoring with tests in place'),
      p('The rule is simple: AI writes, humans review, tests decide.'),
      cta('Have a prototype built with an AI coding tool? We can review it and tell you what to keep, fix or rebuild.', '/solutions/ai-production-engineering')
    ]
  },

  'how-rag-pipelines-work': {
    title: 'How RAG Pipelines Work: A Practical Guide to Production RAG',
    meta_title: 'How RAG Pipelines Work: A Practical Guide to Production RAG',
    meta_description: 'How retrieval-augmented generation works end to end: ingestion, chunking, embeddings, hybrid search, re-ranking, citation and evaluation, and where RAG goes wrong.',
    excerpt: 'A clear walk through each stage of a RAG pipeline, with the decisions that decide whether answers are grounded or made up.',
    category: 'AI engineering', read_time: '9 min read', tags: ['RAG', 'retrieval augmented generation', 'azure openai', 'vector search'],
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('Retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, lets a language model answer using your own documents instead of only what it learned in training. The idea is simple. Making it reliable at scale is not.'),
      p('This guide walks through each stage of a RAG pipeline, explains the decisions that matter, and points out where most systems go wrong.'),
      h('What RAG does'),
      p('A language model does not know your contracts, policies, tickets or product data. RAG fixes that by retrieving the relevant passages at question time and putting them in the prompt, so the model answers from that evidence. Good RAG returns an answer plus the sources it used, so a person can check it.'),
      h('The pipeline, stage by stage'),
      h3('1. Ingestion'),
      p('Content comes from files, databases, SharePoint, wikis, tickets or APIs. Ingestion extracts clean text, handles tables and scanned pages, and records metadata such as source, date, owner and access rules. Poor extraction quietly caps the quality of everything after it.'),
      h3('2. Chunking'),
      p('Documents are split into passages small enough to retrieve precisely but large enough to carry meaning. Splitting on headings and paragraphs usually beats fixed character counts. Adding the document title and section heading to each chunk helps retrieval, and overlapping chunks can preserve context across boundaries.'),
      h3('3. Embeddings and indexing'),
      p('Each chunk is converted to a vector with an embedding model and stored in a vector index, together with its metadata. Choose an embedding model that fits your languages and domain, and plan for re-indexing when you change it. Keep the original text alongside the vector so you can show citations.'),
      h3('4. Retrieval'),
      p('At question time the system finds candidate chunks. Pure vector search handles meaning but can miss exact terms such as product codes or names. Keyword search handles exact terms but misses paraphrases. Hybrid search combines both and is usually the better default. Metadata filters narrow results to the right customer, product or date range.'),
      h3('5. Re-ranking'),
      p('A second model scores the retrieved candidates against the question and reorders them. This often gives the largest quality gain for the least effort, because it fixes cases where the right passage was retrieved but ranked low.'),
      h3('6. Generation with citations'),
      p('The prompt includes the best passages and instructs the model to answer only from them, to cite sources and to say when the answer is not in the material. The system returns the answer with links to the passages, which builds trust and makes errors easy to find.'),
      h3('7. Evaluation and monitoring'),
      p('Build a test set of real questions with expected answers or expected source passages. Measure whether retrieval finds the right passage and whether the answer is faithful to it. Run these tests every time you change chunking, models or prompts, and log live questions that users mark unhelpful.'),
      h('Where RAG systems go wrong'),
      ul('Bad extraction: tables and scanned documents become garbage text.', 'Chunks that are too large, too small or cut mid-thought.', 'No hybrid search, so exact terms are missed.', 'No access control, so users can retrieve documents they should not see.', 'No evaluation, so changes quietly make answers worse.', 'Answers without citations, so errors go unnoticed.', 'Stale indexes that do not update when source documents change.'),
      h('Permissions: the part prototypes skip'),
      p('In a company, not everyone may read every document. A production RAG system must apply the same access rules as the source system at retrieval time, filtering results by the user’s identity and groups. This is often the hardest requirement and the one most demos ignore.'),
      h('Agents and RAG'),
      p('Simple RAG answers one question from one retrieval. Some tasks need several steps: search, read, search again, call a tool, then answer. Agentic RAG lets a model decide those steps. It can answer harder questions, but it adds cost, latency and failure modes, so it needs step limits, tracing and evaluation.'),
      h('Building RAG on Azure'),
      p('A common Azure setup uses Azure OpenAI for generation and embeddings, Azure AI Search for hybrid retrieval and re-ranking, Blob Storage for source files, and Microsoft Entra ID for identity. The same architecture can be built with other providers, and the design decisions above matter more than the product names.'),
      h('Checklist before you ship'),
      ul('Extraction tested on your real documents, including tables and scans', 'Chunking tuned and tested', 'Hybrid retrieval with re-ranking', 'Permission filtering at query time', 'Citations in every answer', 'An evaluation set that runs on each change', 'Index refresh when documents change', 'Logging of questions, retrieved passages and answers'),
      h('Chunking in practice'),
      p('Imagine a 60-page policy document. A fixed split every 1,000 characters will cut sentences and separate a rule from its exception. Splitting by heading keeps each rule with its context. Prepending “Travel policy > Expenses > Meals” to each chunk helps the search find it when someone asks about meal allowances. Tables deserve special care: converting a table to rows with column names, instead of loose text, keeps the numbers attached to their labels.'),
      h('How to evaluate a RAG system'),
      p('Evaluation has two parts. Retrieval evaluation asks whether the right passage appears in the top results for each test question, often reported as recall at a given number of results. Answer evaluation asks whether the answer is correct, whether it is supported by the retrieved passages, and whether it answers the question that was asked. Start with 50 to 100 real questions. Write the expected source for each. Review failures by hand, because they point to the stage that needs work: extraction, chunking, retrieval, ranking or prompting.'),
      h('Cost and latency levers'),
      ul('Retrieve fewer, better passages after re-ranking instead of sending many', 'Use a smaller model for simple questions and a larger one for hard ones', 'Cache answers to repeated questions and repeated retrievals', 'Stream the answer so users see progress', 'Run retrieval steps in parallel where possible'),
      h('RAG or fine-tuning?'),
      p('Fine-tuning changes how a model behaves or writes. RAG gives a model facts it does not have. For company knowledge that changes, RAG is almost always the better first choice: you update documents, not the model, and you can show sources. Fine-tuning can help with tone, format or a narrow repeated task, and it can be combined with RAG.'),
      h('Questions to ask before building'),
      ul('Who will use it and what do they ask?', 'Which sources are authoritative, and how often do they change?', 'Who may see which documents?', 'What happens when the answer is not in the documents?', 'How will we know whether quality is improving?'),
      cta('Planning a RAG system or fixing one that gives weak answers? We build and review RAG pipelines on Azure.', '/solutions/ai-production-engineering')
    ]
  },

  'how-to-pick-ai-stack-startup': {
    title: 'How to Choose an AI Stack for Your Startup',
    meta_title: 'How to Choose an AI Stack for Your Startup',
    meta_description: 'A practical guide to choosing models, vector search, orchestration, hosting and evaluation tools for an AI startup, and how to avoid costly lock-in.',
    excerpt: 'Pick the AI stack in layers: model, retrieval, orchestration, hosting and evaluation. Here is how to decide each one without over-building.',
    category: 'AI engineering', read_time: '8 min read', tags: ['AI stack', 'startups', 'LLM', 'architecture'],
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('Early AI teams often burn weeks choosing tools. The better approach is to pick a simple stack for each layer, keep the layers separable, and spend the saved time on the product and on measuring quality.'),
      p('This guide breaks an AI stack into layers and gives a decision rule for each.'),
      h('Start with the problem, not the tools'),
      p('Write down the task, the users, the data the system needs and what a wrong answer costs. A customer-support assistant, a document extractor and an internal analytics agent need different stacks. The cost of an error decides how much evaluation, review and control you need, and that shapes every other choice.'),
      h('Layer 1: the model'),
      p('Choose a model by testing it on your own task, not by leaderboard. Compare two or three candidates on a small set of real examples for quality, latency and cost. Start with a capable hosted model to find out what is possible, then test whether a smaller or cheaper model meets the quality bar. If you sell to enterprises, check data residency, privacy terms and whether the provider is available through your customers’ cloud, for example Azure OpenAI for Microsoft-centred customers.'),
      h('Layer 2: retrieval and data'),
      p('If the product answers from documents or records, you need retrieval. Start with a managed search or vector service rather than building one. Prefer hybrid search with metadata filters. Keep your source data and your index separate so you can rebuild the index when you change the embedding model.'),
      h('Layer 3: orchestration'),
      p('Orchestration is the code that calls models, tools and retrieval in sequence. For a first version, plain application code with a small amount of structure is often better than a heavy framework. Adopt a framework when you need it, for example for multi-step agents, and keep your prompts and tool definitions in your own files so you can move.'),
      h('Layer 4: hosting and infrastructure'),
      p('Use the platform your team already knows and your customers accept. A managed platform for web apps and APIs, a managed database and a queue for background jobs cover most startups. Add container orchestration only when you have a reason. If enterprise customers matter, plan for single sign-on, audit logs and regional data requirements early.'),
      h('Layer 5: evaluation and monitoring'),
      p('This is the layer teams skip and regret. Create an evaluation set of real tasks with expected outcomes from the first week, score outputs automatically where possible, and rerun the set whenever you change a prompt, model or retrieval setting. In production, trace each request and track cost, latency and user feedback.'),
      h('Avoiding lock-in without over-engineering'),
      ul('Put model calls behind one internal function so switching models is a small change.', 'Keep prompts in version-controlled files, not scattered in code.', 'Store raw documents separately from embeddings.', 'Avoid features that exist in only one vendor unless they are central to your product.', 'Log inputs and outputs so you can test a new model on real traffic.'),
      h('A sensible default stack for a first version'),
      ul('A hosted frontier model, tested against one cheaper alternative', 'A managed hybrid search service if you need retrieval', 'Application code for orchestration, with a framework only for complex agents', 'A managed web platform and database you already know', 'An evaluation set and request tracing from day one'),
      h('When to revisit the stack'),
      p('Revisit when cost per task threatens your margin, when quality plateaus on your evaluation set, when a customer’s security requirements demand a change, or when a new model changes what is possible. Because you kept the layers separable and you have an evaluation set, each of these is a measured change instead of a rewrite.'),
      h('A simple way to test models on your task'),
      p('Collect 30 to 50 real examples of the task with the output you want. Run each candidate model on all of them with the same prompt. Score the results by hand or with an automatic check, and record latency and cost per example. Repeat after improving the prompt. This takes a day or two and replaces weeks of opinion.'),
      h('Three example stacks'),
      h3('A document-extraction product'),
      p('A hosted model with strong structured-output support, a queue for background jobs, object storage for files and a database for results. Retrieval is rarely needed. Evaluation focuses on field-level accuracy against labelled documents.'),
      h3('A customer-support assistant'),
      p('A hosted model, hybrid search over help articles and past tickets, citations in answers, a hand-off to a human agent, and evaluation on real tickets. Permissions matter if the knowledge base has internal-only content.'),
      h3('An agent that updates business systems'),
      p('A model with reliable tool calling, an orchestration layer with step limits, approval points before writes, full tracing and a sandbox environment. Security and audit come before features.'),
      h('Estimating cost before you scale'),
      p('Estimate tokens per task: the prompt, retrieved context and answer. Multiply by tasks per user per month and by the price of the model. Add retrieval, hosting and logging. Compare the result with what each user pays you. If the margin is thin, look at a smaller model, shorter context, caching or a usage-based price.'),
      h('Mistakes we see most often'),
      ul('Choosing a framework before understanding the problem', 'Using the largest model for every step', 'No evaluation set, so nobody knows whether a change helped', 'Mixing prompts into application code', 'Ignoring enterprise requirements until a big customer asks'),
      cta('Choosing an AI stack for a new product? We can review your architecture and help you pick what to build and what to buy.', '/solutions/ai-product-engineering')
    ]
  },

  'rag-as-a-service-build-buy-or-partner': {
    title: 'RAG as a Service: Build, Buy or Partner?',
    meta_title: 'RAG as a Service: Build, Buy or Partner?',
    meta_description: 'Compare managed RAG services, a custom RAG build and working with an engineering partner. When each makes sense, and what to ask before you choose.',
    excerpt: 'Managed RAG, custom RAG or an engineering partner: a decision guide with the questions that settle it.',
    category: 'AI engineering', read_time: '7 min read', tags: ['RAG as a service', 'RAG development', 'build vs buy', 'AI engineering'],
    published_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', featured: false, icon: 'search', ...author,
    body: [
      i('Teams that want AI to answer questions from their own documents face three routes: use a managed RAG service, build a custom pipeline, or bring in an engineering partner. Each fits different situations.'),
      h('What “RAG as a service” usually means'),
      p('Managed RAG services let you upload or connect documents and get a question-answering interface or API without building the pipeline. They handle ingestion, indexing and retrieval for you. They are quick to start and need little engineering.'),
      h('When a managed service is the right choice'),
      ul('Your content is mostly standard documents and web pages.', 'You need an internal tool soon and can accept the service’s limits.', 'The stakes of a wrong answer are low and users can check sources.', 'You have little engineering capacity.'),
      p('Limits to check: how it handles tables and scans, whether it supports your access rules, what you can tune, where data is stored, how you evaluate quality, and what happens to cost as usage grows.'),
      h('When a custom RAG build is worth it'),
      ul('RAG is part of your product, so quality is a competitive feature.', 'You need to enforce per-user permissions from source systems.', 'Your documents are complex: tables, forms, drawings, multilingual content.', 'You need control over chunking, ranking, models and cost.', 'Compliance requires specific data handling or hosting.'),
      p('Custom builds cost more up front. They pay back when answer quality, control or integration decides whether the feature works.'),
      h('When to bring in an engineering partner'),
      p('A partner makes sense when you need a custom build but lack the experience or capacity, when a managed service has hit its limits, or when an existing RAG system gives weak answers and nobody knows why. A good partner starts by measuring: an evaluation set, a review of retrieval quality and a clear list of fixes.'),
      h('Questions to settle the decision'),
      ul('How costly is a wrong answer?', 'Do different users have different access to documents?', 'How messy and varied are the sources?', 'Is RAG a feature of your product or an internal tool?', 'Who will operate and improve it after launch?', 'What are your data residency and security requirements?', 'What is the expected query volume and cost per answer?'),
      h('A common path'),
      p('Many teams start with a managed service to learn what users ask, measure where answers fail, then move the parts that matter to a custom pipeline. Keep your documents and your evaluation questions in your own hands from the start so that move is easy.'),
      h('What to ask any vendor or partner'),
      ul('How do you evaluate answer quality, and can we see the results?', 'How are permissions enforced at retrieval time?', 'Can we export our data and index?', 'What does cost look like at ten times today’s usage?', 'How are citations produced?', 'What is your process when answers are wrong?'),
      h('What drives cost in each route'),
      h3('Managed service'),
      p('Cost usually scales with documents stored and questions asked. It is low at the start and can grow quickly with usage. Ask for a clear price at ten times your current volume.'),
      h3('Custom build'),
      p('Cost is mostly engineering time at the start, then hosting, model usage and maintenance. You control the levers: model choice, retrieval depth and caching.'),
      h3('Partner'),
      p('Cost depends on scope. A review and evaluation phase is small and gives you facts. Building a full pipeline is larger. Agree the phases up front so you can stop after each one.'),
      h('Typical timelines by route'),
      p('A managed service can be live in days. A custom pipeline for clean documents can be a working first version in a few weeks, with further time spent on evaluation, permissions and hardening. Timelines depend heavily on document quality and integration needs, so ask any partner what they need to give a firm estimate.'),
      h('Signs you have outgrown a managed service'),
      ul('Users report wrong or missing answers and you cannot tune them', 'You need per-user permissions the service cannot apply', 'Costs rise faster than value', 'You need answers inside your own product with your own design', 'A customer asks where their data lives and you cannot answer'),
      h('Questions that reveal a good partner'),
      p('Ask how they would measure quality in the first two weeks. A good answer starts with your questions, an evaluation set and a review of retrieval, not with a tool choice.'),
      cta('Not sure whether to build, buy or partner for RAG? Talk to us about your documents, users and requirements.', '/solutions/ai-production-engineering')
    ]
  },

  'azure-openai-on-your-data-vs-custom-rag': {
    title: 'Azure OpenAI On Your Data vs a Custom RAG Pipeline',
    meta_title: 'Azure OpenAI On Your Data vs a Custom RAG Pipeline',
    meta_description: 'Azure OpenAI On Your Data is the quick route to chat over your documents. When it is enough, and when a custom RAG pipeline on Azure AI Search is the better choice.',
    excerpt: 'Use the built-in route when it fits, and build a custom pipeline when quality, permissions or control demand it.',
    category: 'Azure', read_time: '7 min read', tags: ['azure openai on your data', 'azure ai search', 'RAG', 'azure openai'],
    published_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', featured: false, icon: 'cloud', ...author,
    body: [
      i('Microsoft offers a built-in way to ground Azure OpenAI chat models on your data, often called Azure OpenAI On Your Data. It connects a model to a data source such as Azure AI Search so users can chat over documents with little custom code. A custom RAG pipeline gives you more control. This guide compares the two.'),
      p('Features and limits change, so check current Microsoft documentation before you decide. The trade-offs below are stable.'),
      h('What the built-in route gives you'),
      ul('A fast path to a working chat over your documents', 'Less code to write and maintain', 'Integration with Azure AI Search and Azure identity', 'Citations back to source documents', 'A good way to validate demand before investing more'),
      h('Where it can fall short'),
      ul('Limited control over chunking, ranking and prompts compared with your own pipeline', 'Complex documents such as tables, forms and scans may need custom extraction', 'Per-user permission rules may need custom design', 'Evaluation, tracing and cost controls are yours to add', 'Less flexibility for multi-step or agent workflows'),
      h('What a custom RAG pipeline adds'),
      p('With a custom pipeline you design ingestion and chunking for your documents, tune hybrid retrieval and re-ranking in Azure AI Search, apply your own prompt and citation format, filter by user identity, and add evaluation and tracing. You also choose when to use agents. The cost is more engineering up front and more to maintain.'),
      h('How to decide'),
      ul('If the use case is an internal tool over fairly clean documents, start with the built-in route.', 'If answer quality is a product feature, build custom.', 'If different users must see different documents, plan custom permission filtering.', 'If documents are messy or multilingual, expect custom extraction.', 'If you need agents or multi-step workflows, build custom orchestration.'),
      h('A staged approach'),
      p('Many teams use the built-in route to prove value, collect real questions, and measure failures. Those questions become the evaluation set for a custom pipeline if the limits start to matter. Keep source documents in your own storage and keep the question log so moving is simple.'),
      h('Security and governance on Azure'),
      p('Either route should use Microsoft Entra ID for access, managed identities rather than keys where possible, network controls for the services, content filtering, and logging. Decide early where prompts and responses are stored and who can read them.'),
      h('A typical Azure architecture for a custom pipeline'),
      p('Source files sit in Blob Storage or SharePoint. An ingestion job extracts text, splits it, creates embeddings with an Azure OpenAI embedding model and writes chunks with metadata to an Azure AI Search index. At query time the application authenticates the user with Entra ID, builds a filter from the user’s groups, runs hybrid search with semantic ranking, passes the top passages to an Azure OpenAI chat model and returns the answer with citations. Tracing goes to your monitoring service.'),
      h('Designing permissions'),
      p('The usual approach is to store access information, such as group identifiers, on each chunk in the index and filter by the user’s groups at query time. This is called security trimming. It needs a plan for keeping the index in step when permissions change in the source system.'),
      h('Prompt and citation design'),
      p('Tell the model to answer only from the supplied passages, to cite them and to say when the answer is missing. Give each passage an identifier the model can reference, then turn those references into links for the user.'),
      h('Moving from the built-in route to custom'),
      ul('Keep your source documents in your own storage', 'Log user questions and which answers were weak', 'Turn the weak questions into an evaluation set', 'Rebuild retrieval and ranking in the custom pipeline and compare against the baseline', 'Switch over when the custom pipeline is measurably better'),
      h('Cost considerations'),
      p('The main costs are model tokens, search service capacity and embeddings for indexing. A custom pipeline lets you reduce tokens by sending fewer, better passages and by choosing a smaller model for simple questions.'),
      cta('Need Azure OpenAI over your internal documents? We can help you pick the route and build it.', '/capabilities/azure')
    ]
  },

  'white-label-software-development-checklist-for-agencies': {
    title: 'White-Label Software Development: A Checklist for Agencies',
    meta_title: 'White-Label Software Development: A Checklist for Agencies',
    meta_description: 'Using a white-label development partner? A practical checklist for agencies: NDA and IP, communication, process, security, quality and handover.',
    excerpt: 'Settle these points before the first sprint and a white-label partnership runs smoothly.',
    category: 'Agencies', read_time: '6 min read', tags: ['white label software development', 'agency', 'outsourcing', 'subcontract'],
    published_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', featured: false, icon: 'users', ...author,
    body: [
      i('Agencies often sell software projects they cannot staff alone. A white-label development partner fills the gap, but the arrangement only works when expectations are clear. Use this checklist before the first sprint.'),
      h('1. Legal and ownership'),
      ul('A mutual NDA covering your agency, your client and the partner', 'IP assignment so code belongs to you or your client', 'Clear terms on use of open-source and third-party components', 'Liability, warranty and support terms that match what you promise your client'),
      h('2. Visibility and client contact'),
      ul('Agree whether the partner is ever named or introduced', 'Decide who communicates with the client and how', 'Agree how partner team members appear in tools and email'),
      h('3. Process and tools'),
      ul('Work in your repositories, project tracker and chat', 'Agree branching, review and release practices', 'Define what “done” means, including tests and documentation', 'Set a regular rhythm: planning, stand-ups, demos'),
      h('4. Scope and change'),
      ul('Write acceptance criteria for each piece of work', 'Agree how estimates are produced and revised', 'Define a change-request process and who approves it', 'Plan how risks and blockers are raised'),
      h('5. Security and data'),
      ul('Limit access to what the project needs', 'Use your or your client’s environments and secrets management', 'Agree rules for personal data, including test data', 'Require secure devices and accounts for anyone with access'),
      h('6. Quality'),
      ul('Code review on every change', 'Automated tests on critical paths', 'A staging environment for acceptance', 'A bug-fix policy for the warranty period'),
      h('7. Communication across time zones'),
      ul('Agree overlapping hours for daily contact', 'Use written updates so progress is visible without meetings', 'Name a single point of contact on each side'),
      h('8. Handover'),
      ul('Documented architecture, setup and deployment steps', 'Runbooks for common issues', 'Transfer of repositories, accounts and credentials', 'A support arrangement after launch'),
      h('Start small'),
      p('The safest way to begin is a small scoped project. It tests communication, quality and fit before you commit a larger client project to the partner.'),
      h('Engagement models agencies use'),
      ul('Project-based: a defined scope delivered by the partner', 'Dedicated team: engineers assigned to your agency over months', 'Overflow: extra capacity for peak periods', 'Specialist: a partner brought in for a skill your team lacks, such as AI or Azure'),
      h('Red flags when choosing a partner'),
      ul('Reluctance to sign an NDA or assign IP', 'No clear process for review and testing', 'Estimates without questions about scope', 'One person who holds all the knowledge', 'No references or examples of comparable work', 'Vague answers about security'),
      h('What a good first 30 days looks like'),
      p('Week one: contracts, access and a joint kickoff. Week two: a small but real piece of work, reviewed by your team. Week three: a demo and a retrospective on communication and quality. Week four: an agreed plan for the next phase, or a clean stop. Starting this way shows you how the partner behaves under real conditions before the stakes rise.'),
      h('Protecting your client relationship'),
      p('Keep the client contact, commercial terms and final acceptance on your side. Keep documentation and credentials in accounts you control. Give the partner the access they need and no more.'),
      cta('Looking for a white-label development partner for AI, web or Azure projects? See how we work with agencies.', '/solutions/agency-development-partner')
    ]
  },

  'production-ready-ai-checklist': {
    title: 'What Production-Ready AI Means: A 12-Point Checklist',
    meta_title: 'What Production-Ready AI Means: A 12-Point Checklist',
    meta_description: 'A 12-point checklist for production-ready AI: access control, data isolation, retrieval quality, evaluation, cost, latency, monitoring, security and ownership.',
    excerpt: 'Use this checklist to see how far an AI prototype is from production.',
    category: 'AI engineering', read_time: '7 min read', tags: ['production AI', 'AI engineering', 'LLM operations', 'checklist'],
    published_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z', featured: false, icon: 'check', ...author,
    body: [
      i('An AI demo shows that a model can do something useful. A production AI system does it reliably, safely and affordably for real users. This checklist covers what separates the two.'),
      h('Access and data'),
      h3('1. Users, roles and permissions'),
      p('Know who can use the system and what each role may see or do. Enforce it on the server and in the data layer, not only in the interface.'),
      h3('2. Data isolation'),
      p('If you serve several customers or business units, make sure data never crosses between them, in storage, in retrieval and in prompts.'),
      h3('3. Data handling'),
      p('Decide what data is sent to models, where it is stored, how long it is kept and how personal data is protected. Align this with your customers’ requirements.'),
      h('Quality'),
      h3('4. Retrieval quality'),
      p('If the system answers from your data, test retrieval on real questions and return citations so errors can be found.'),
      h3('5. Evaluation set'),
      p('Keep a set of real tasks with expected outcomes and run it on every change to prompts, models or retrieval. Without it, quality drifts unseen.'),
      h3('6. Human review where it matters'),
      p('Place review and approval points where a wrong output is costly: money, safety, legal or customer-facing commitments.'),
      h('Reliability and cost'),
      h3('7. Failure handling'),
      p('Plan for timeouts, rate limits and outages from model providers and other dependencies, with retries, fallbacks and clear messages to users.'),
      h3('8. Latency'),
      p('Set a target response time, measure it and tune with caching, smaller models, streaming and parallel calls.'),
      h3('9. Cost control'),
      p('Measure tokens and cost per task, set budgets and alerts, and limit runaway loops in agents.'),
      h('Security and operations'),
      h3('10. Security review'),
      p('Cover secrets, prompt injection, tool permissions, logging of sensitive data and access to model outputs.'),
      h3('11. Monitoring and tracing'),
      p('Trace prompts, retrieved passages, tool calls and outputs, and watch error rates, latency and user feedback after release.'),
      h3('12. Ownership'),
      p('Name who operates the system, reviews evaluation results and decides when to update models or prompts.'),
      h('How to use the list'),
      p('Score each point as ready, partly ready or missing. The missing items are your production backlog. Fix security and data issues first, then reliability, then quality tooling and cost.'),
      h('A scoring example'),
      p('Take a support assistant built on a language model. Users and roles: ready. Data isolation: partly ready, because documents from two customers share one index. Retrieval quality: partly ready, with no test set. Evaluation: missing. Human review: ready for refunds. Failure handling: missing, so a provider timeout shows a blank screen. Cost: missing. Security review: partly ready. Monitoring: missing. The backlog is clear: isolate data first, then add evaluation, failure handling and monitoring.'),
      h('Who should own each point'),
      ul('Engineering: access, isolation, failure handling, latency, monitoring', 'Product: review points, quality bar and evaluation set content', 'Security: data handling and security review', 'Finance or operations: cost budgets and alerts', 'A named lead: the overall operation of the system'),
      h('How often to repeat the review'),
      p('Run the checklist before launch, after any major change to models or data sources, and at regular intervals such as every quarter. Models, providers and usage patterns change, and so does the risk.'),
      h('Common mistakes'),
      ul('Treating the model as the whole system', 'Launching without a test set', 'Letting costs grow unobserved', 'Adding agents without limits or approvals', 'Leaving ownership unclear'),
      cta('Want a production-readiness review of your AI system? We can assess it against this list and give you a prioritised plan.', '/solutions/ai-production-engineering')
    ]
  },
  'ai-medical-scribe-azure-openai': {
    meta_title: 'AI Medical Scribe on Azure OpenAI: Architecture and Lessons',
    meta_description: 'How an AI medical scribe works on Azure OpenAI and Azure Speech: pipeline, specialty prompts, FHIR and EHR integration, and how to earn physician trust.',
    excerpt: 'Clinical documentation takes up a large share of a physician\'s day. This is how an Azure OpenAI medical scribe is built, and where the real effort goes.',
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('Clinical documentation consumes 30 to 40 percent of a physician\'s day. AI medical scribes built on Azure OpenAI change that equation. We have deployed this approach in several healthcare settings, and the pattern is consistent: faster notes, fewer errors and physicians who finish on time.'),
      p('This article explains what an AI scribe does, the architecture we use, and the parts that take the most time in practice: prompts, EHR integration and physician trust.'),
      h('What an AI scribe actually does'),
      p('An AI medical scribe listens to the patient and physician conversation in real time, extracts the clinical intent and drafts structured notes: SOAP notes, history of present illness, assessment and plan. The physician reviews the draft and it is inserted into the EHR. This is comprehension, not transcription. A transcript records what was said; a scribe decides what belongs in the chart and where.'),
      h('The Azure OpenAI architecture'),
      p('We use Azure OpenAI for note generation and Azure AI Speech for real-time audio. The pipeline runs in this order:'),
      ul('Audio streams from the clinic device to Azure AI Speech', 'The transcript is produced in chunks as the visit progresses', 'A specialty-aware prompt turns the transcript into a structured clinical note', 'The note is reviewed and edited by the physician', 'The approved note is written to the EHR through its API'),
      p('The services involved are HIPAA-eligible Azure services, and the design keeps data inside the customer\'s tenant boundary. Eligibility is a starting point, not a guarantee, so configuration, access control and logging still need to be set up correctly. Our guide to building HIPAA-compliant AI covers that in detail.'),
      h('Prompt engineering for clinical accuracy'),
      p('Generic prompts produce generic notes. A cardiology note looks very different from a psychiatry note, so we build specialty-specific templates and keep them versioned. We also pass patient context into the prompt, such as age, chief complaint and existing conditions, which improves extraction because the model no longer has to guess what the visit is about.'),
      h3('What good prompt design includes'),
      ul('Separate templates per specialty and visit type', 'Explicit instructions on what to leave out when the conversation did not cover it', 'A rule that the model must not invent findings, doses or diagnoses', 'Structured output so downstream systems can validate every field'),
      h('EHR integration realities'),
      p('FHIR R4 is the standard, but real EHR APIs are inconsistent. We have integrated with Epic, Athenahealth and several smaller systems, and each one needed custom mapping. Plan for this early. It is where most of the integration time goes, and it is rarely visible in a demo.'),
      h('Accuracy and physician trust'),
      p('Physicians do not trust black boxes. We built a review-and-edit interface that shows confidence by section, highlights uncertain extractions and tracks how often each section is edited. Edit rates give you a real accuracy measure instead of an impression. As the system learns from corrections, accuracy improves, and most clinicians reach 90 percent or better accuracy within two weeks of use.'),
      h('Questions to settle before you build'),
      ul('Which specialties launch first, and who owns their templates?', 'Which EHRs must you integrate with, and who provides sandbox access?', 'Where is audio stored, for how long, and who can replay it?', 'How will you measure accuracy and edit rates after launch?', 'What is the fallback when speech or the model is unavailable?'),
      cta('Building a healthcare AI product? We have done this before and can help you scope it.', '/solutions/healthcare-ai-automation')
    ]
  },
  'hipaa-compliant-ai-healthcare-startups': {
    meta_title: 'HIPAA-Compliant AI for Healthcare Startups: A Build Guide',
    meta_description: 'What HIPAA requires of AI systems, why a BAA is not enough, which Azure services are eligible, and how de-identification and audit logging fit in.',
    excerpt: 'HIPAA is an architecture you build in, not a feature you add late. Here is what it means for AI systems, and how to plan for it from day one.',
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('Every week a healthcare startup asks us to add HIPAA compliance late in development. It usually ends the same way: expensive rework, a delayed launch and a nervous legal team. HIPAA is not a feature you bolt on. It is an architecture you build in from the start.'),
      p('This guide covers what HIPAA means for AI systems in practice, what a Business Associate Agreement does and does not do, and how to design for compliance on Azure. It is engineering guidance, not legal advice, so involve your compliance counsel.'),
      h('What HIPAA requires of AI systems'),
      p('The Security Rule requires technical safeguards: access controls, audit controls, integrity controls and transmission security. For AI systems this translates into a few concrete rules:'),
      ul('No PHI in model prompts unless the provider and your controls allow it', 'Encrypted storage and encrypted transport everywhere', 'Granular role-based access to data and to AI features', 'Complete audit trails of who did what, and what the AI did'),
      h('The BAA: necessary but not sufficient'),
      p('A Business Associate Agreement with your cloud provider is necessary. It covers the vendor\'s obligations, but it does not make your application compliant. Your code, your access rules, your logging and your staff processes are still your responsibility. Teams that treat the signed BAA as the finish line are the ones that fail audits.'),
      h('Azure\'s HIPAA-eligible services'),
      p('Azure offers a broad set of HIPAA-eligible services. For AI workloads the common building blocks are Azure OpenAI, Azure AI Speech, Azure Blob Storage, Azure SQL Database and Azure Key Vault. Eligibility depends on how a service is used and under which agreement, so confirm the current list and your configuration against Microsoft\'s documentation before you rely on it.'),
      h('De-identification as a strategy'),
      p('Where you can, de-identify data before it reaches an AI model. Azure\'s Text Analytics for Health includes PHI detection and de-identification. Processing de-identified data removes or substantially reduces HIPAA obligations for that step, and it limits the damage if something goes wrong. Many workloads, such as summarising trends or classifying document types, do not need names or identifiers at all.'),
      h('Audit logging for AI'),
      p('Traditional audit logging records who accessed a record. AI audit logging needs more. Capture which model and version was called, what input was sent, what output came back and what downstream action followed. Without this you cannot answer a regulator, or your own clinician, when asked why the system did something.'),
      h3('A practical logging checklist'),
      ul('User and role for every request', 'Model name, version and prompt template version', 'Input and output references, stored securely', 'Downstream action taken and who approved it', 'Retention periods that match your policy'),
      h('Design decisions to make in week one'),
      ul('Which data is PHI, and where does it flow?', 'Can any AI step run on de-identified data?', 'Which regions and tenants will hold the data?', 'Who has access to production data, and how is it logged?', 'What is the incident and breach response process?'),
      cta('Building healthcare AI? We have worked through HIPAA requirements on several products and can review your design.', '/industries/healthcare')
    ]
  },
  'fax-to-ai-healthcare-automation': {
    meta_title: 'Fax to AI: Automating Healthcare Document Intake',
    meta_description: 'How an AI pipeline turns inbound healthcare faxes into classified, routed records using Azure Document Intelligence and GPT-4, with humans in the loop.',
    excerpt: 'Healthcare still runs on fax. Here is the AI pipeline that reads, classifies and routes inbound faxes without removing human oversight.',
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('In 2025, US healthcare still sends roughly nine billion faxes a year. We built an AI system that does not fight fax culture. It transforms it.'),
      p('This article describes the problem, the pipeline, how classification works at volume and why we keep a human in the loop for clinical decisions.'),
      h('The problem with healthcare fax'),
      p('Inbound faxes arrive as images. Staff print them, read them, re-enter the data into the EHR and route them to the right clinician. That takes five to fifteen minutes per fax. The work is repetitive, error-prone and invisible until it falls behind, and then referrals and lab results wait.'),
      h('The AI pipeline'),
      p('The pipeline runs from receipt to EHR in under 90 seconds:'),
      ul('Inbound fax arrives through an eFax API', 'Azure Document Intelligence performs OCR on the image', 'GPT-4 extracts clinical entities from the text', 'The document is classified by type', 'A routing rules engine decides where it goes', 'The data is written to the EHR through integration'),
      h('Document classification at scale'),
      p('Healthcare faxes include referrals, lab results, prior authorisations, prescription refill requests, medical records and insurance correspondence. Each needs different downstream handling, so classification is the step that makes everything else possible. Current accuracy is 94 percent. We treat the remaining cases as a design input, not an embarrassment: low-confidence documents go to a person.'),
      h3('Why OCR quality matters'),
      p('Faxes are low resolution, skewed and often handwritten in places. OCR errors flow into extraction, so we measure quality at each stage separately instead of reporting a single accuracy number that hides where errors begin.'),
      h('Human-in-the-loop design'),
      p('We never route fully autonomously for clinical decisions. The AI extracts and classifies, and humans confirm and act. Review screens show the original image next to the extracted fields, with uncertain values highlighted. Volume capacity per staff member increased eightfold, because people spend their time on judgement instead of retyping.'),
      h('What to define before you automate'),
      ul('Which document types you receive, and in what volumes', 'Which types can be auto-filed and which always need review', 'The confidence threshold that triggers human review', 'How exceptions and misroutes are corrected and fed back', 'Who owns the routing rules as workflows change'),
      cta('Running a healthcare operation drowning in faxes? Let us look at your intake and scope an automation.', '/solutions/healthcare-ai-automation')
    ]
  },
  'ai-voice-agents-vs-call-centres': {
    meta_title: 'AI Voice Agents vs Call Centres: What Each Does Best',
    meta_description: 'Which calls AI voice agents handle well, where humans still win, how to keep latency low and how to design the AI-to-human handoff.',
    excerpt: 'The real question is not whether voice AI replaces call centres. It is which calls AI handles, which escalate, and how the handoff works.',
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('The question is not whether AI voice agents will replace call centres. It is which calls the AI handles, which calls escalate to humans, and how you architect the handoff between them.'),
      p('This article sets out where voice agents perform well, where people still win, what makes a voice conversation feel natural and the hybrid design we recommend.'),
      h('What AI voice agents do well'),
      p('Voice agents handle structured, repeatable calls: appointment scheduling, FAQ responses, order status, simple account questions, outbound reminders and intake collection. For most businesses these categories make up 60 to 80 percent of inbound call volume, so even partial automation changes capacity and wait times.'),
      h('Where humans still win'),
      ul('Emotionally complex calls where tone and empathy matter', 'Novel situations outside what the system was designed for', 'High-stakes decisions that need human accountability', 'Any call where the customer asks for a person'),
      p('Treat the last item as a rule, not a failure. A voice agent that blocks a request to speak to a human damages trust faster than any wrong answer.'),
      h('The latency problem'),
      p('People notice pauses. Modern architectures reduce latency with streaming text-to-speech, smaller intent-detection models for routing and pre-cached responses for common intents. This brings response time to around 300 to 500 milliseconds, within human conversational tolerance. Design for it from the start, because adding speed later usually means changing the architecture.'),
      h3('What affects perceived speed'),
      ul('Streaming audio instead of waiting for a full response', 'Using a fast model for routing and a larger one only when needed', 'Caching answers to the most common questions', 'Handling interruptions so callers can talk over the agent'),
      h('Hybrid architecture: the sensible approach'),
      p('The winning pattern is AI first with intelligent escalation. The AI handles the opening, collects context and resolves simple cases on its own. For complex cases it transfers to a human agent with the full context pre-loaded, so the caller never repeats themselves. Done well, humans spend their time on the calls that need them, and customers get faster answers on the rest.'),
      h('Questions to answer before you start'),
      ul('Which call types make up most of your volume?', 'What are the escalation triggers, and how fast is the handoff?', 'What systems must the agent read from and write to?', 'How will you review recordings and measure resolution quality?', 'What consent and disclosure rules apply in your markets?'),
      cta('Want to see what AI voice could look like in your operation? Talk to our voice AI team.', '/solutions/ai-product-engineering')
    ]
  },
  'building-bilingual-ai-middle-east': {
    meta_title: 'Building Bilingual AI for the Middle East: Arabic and English',
    meta_description: 'Lessons from a bilingual Arabic and English medical scribe: dialects, GPT-4 and Arabic, right-to-left UI design and Saudi cloud compliance.',
    excerpt: 'Lessons from building a bilingual AI medical scribe for the Saudi market: dialects, RTL design and where the data has to live.',
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('We built a bilingual AI medical scribe for the Saudi healthcare market, with simultaneous English and Arabic support, a right-to-left interface and dialect-aware language processing. It was one of the most technically interesting projects we have shipped.'),
      p('This article covers what we learned about Arabic dialects, language model performance, RTL interface design and compliance when building AI for Middle East markets.'),
      h('Arabic is not one language'),
      p('Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) differs substantially from Gulf, Levantine and Egyptian Arabic, among others. A medical scribe serving Saudi clinicians must understand Gulf dialect speech while producing clinical documentation in MSA. Treating Arabic as a single language is the most common early mistake, and it shows up as poor transcription.'),
      h('GPT-4 and Arabic: better than expected'),
      p('GPT-4 Turbo handles MSA very well. Our approach combined three steps:'),
      ul('Azure Speech Services for dialect-aware transcription', 'Post-processing to normalise dialect output to MSA', 'GPT-4 for generating the clinical note'),
      p('Separating transcription, normalisation and generation let us test and improve each stage on its own, which matters when you have to explain errors to clinicians.'),
      h('RTL UI: more than text direction'),
      p('Right-to-left design is not just flipping text direction. Date formats, number rendering, icon placement, progress indicators and form flow all need to be reconsidered. Mixed content is the hard part: an Arabic sentence containing an English drug name or a numeric value must still read correctly. Test with real bilingual content early, not at the end.'),
      h3('RTL checks worth doing'),
      ul('Mirrored layouts, navigation and progress indicators', 'Correct handling of mixed Arabic, English and numbers', 'Date and number formats that match local expectations', 'Icons with direction, such as arrows, flipped where needed', 'Fonts that render Arabic clearly at small sizes'),
      h('Saudi cloud compliance'),
      p('Healthcare data in Saudi Arabia falls under the Health Data Management Regulation (HDMR). Azure\'s UAE North and Saudi Arabia regions are the relevant deployment targets. Check current regional availability and your legal obligations with counsel before you choose a region, because both can change.'),
      h('Planning a Middle East launch'),
      ul('Which dialects and which written standard does the product need?', 'Who will review Arabic output for clinical and linguistic accuracy?', 'Which region will hold the data, and what does the regulation require?', 'Does the interface need to support both directions at once?'),
      cta('Building for MENA markets? We have on-ground experience and can help you scope it.', '/contact')
    ]
  },
  'ai-in-edtech-2025': {
    meta_title: 'AI in EdTech 2025: What Works and What Does Not',
    meta_description: 'An honest look at AI in education: adaptive assessment, writing feedback, AI tutors, and the applications that are not working, such as essay detectors.',
    excerpt: 'After the 2023 to 2024 hype cycle, the picture for AI in education is more nuanced and more encouraging. Here is what is working.',
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('EdTech went through an AI hype cycle in 2023 and 2024 that produced a lot of AI-wrapped content delivery and very little real improvement in learning. In 2025 the picture is more nuanced, and more encouraging.'),
      p('This article separates the applications with evidence behind them from those that are uneven or failing, so product teams can invest in the right ones.'),
      h('Adaptive assessment: the quiet success story'),
      p('AI-powered adaptive assessment has clear evidence behind it. Platforms using this approach report 15 to 25 percent improvement in assessment completion rates and better predictive validity for learning outcomes. The reason it works is simple: the system chooses the next question based on what the learner has shown, so assessments are shorter and more informative.'),
      h('Automated writing feedback that helps'),
      p('GPT-4 class models make substantive feedback possible: argument structure, evidence quality, logical consistency and discipline-specific conventions. When it is built as a formative tool, giving feedback before final submission, improvements in writing quality are measurable. It works best when the learner revises, not when the tool simply rewrites.'),
      h('AI tutors: promising but uneven'),
      p('AI tutors work best for procedural subjects with clear right and wrong answers, such as mathematics, coding and language grammar. Socratic tutoring for the humanities is harder, because quality depends on judgement, nuance and context that models handle inconsistently.'),
      h3('Where tutors tend to succeed'),
      ul('Clear learning objectives and checkable answers', 'Step-by-step hints instead of final answers', 'Content grounded in the course material', 'Teacher visibility into what learners asked'),
      h('What is not working'),
      p('AI essay detectors are unreliable and raise serious fairness concerns, including false accusations against students. The EdTech AI applications that succeed are those that augment educators, not replace them.'),
      h('Questions for an EdTech AI roadmap'),
      ul('Which learning outcome are we improving, and how will we measure it?', 'Does the feature support teachers or bypass them?', 'What happens when the AI is wrong, and who sees it?', 'How are learner data and privacy protected, especially for minors?'),
      cta('Building an EdTech product? We have shipped AI learning platforms and can help you plan yours.', '/contact')
    ]
  },
  'pet-care-tech-rfid-ai': {
    meta_title: 'RFID and AI for Pet Care: Vaccination Camp Platform',
    meta_description: 'How RFID microchip scanning, offline-first Android tablets and Azure replaced paper registers at animal vaccination camps, with automated booster reminders.',
    excerpt: 'Paper registers at vaccination camps lose records and miss boosters. This is the RFID and cloud platform that replaced them.',
    updated_at: '2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z',
    body: [
      i('Vaccination camps across India process thousands of animals a year with paper registers, manual data entry and phone-based follow-ups. We built a cloud-native platform that replaces this with RFID scanning, real-time dashboards and AI-powered health trend analysis.'),
      p('This article explains the problem, the RFID foundation, the offline-first design and how automated reminders improved booster compliance.'),
      h('The paper problem'),
      p('Manual processes at vaccination camps cause real harm: lost records, wrong animal identification, missed booster reminders and no aggregate data for disease surveillance. Without reliable records you cannot know which animals are protected or where an outbreak is starting.'),
      h('RFID as the foundation'),
      p('Scanning ISO 11784/11785 microchips gives reliable animal identification. The intake process drops from eight minutes to under two, and misidentification incidents are effectively eliminated. Everything else in the platform depends on getting identity right, so we started there.'),
      h('Cloud architecture for intermittent connectivity'),
      p('Camps often run where internet access is unreliable. We built the system offline-first. Data is captured locally on rugged Android tablets and synced to Azure whenever a connection is available. Staff never wait for the network, and no record is lost because a signal dropped.'),
      h3('Offline-first design principles'),
      ul('Every action works without a connection', 'Sync is automatic, resumable and safe to repeat', 'Conflicts are resolved by clear, predictable rules', 'Staff can see what has and has not been synced'),
      h('Automated booster reminders'),
      p('Because every animal now has a reliable record, the platform can remind owners when boosters are due. Booster compliance rates improved by 340 percent in the pilot area within 12 months of deployment. Aggregate data also gives health officials a real-time view of vaccination coverage and trends.'),
      h('Planning a field-data platform'),
      ul('What identifier will you use, and who can scan it?', 'What happens at a site with no connectivity for a full day?', 'Who needs dashboards, and what decisions do they make?', 'How will owners be reminded, and through which channel?'),
      cta('Building pet care or veterinary technology? See how we approach field-ready platforms.', '/contact')
    ]
  }
};
